 |\n| MIT | left-pad==0.2 |\n| GPL-3.0 | somelib==1.1 |\n\n**Flagged: `somelib==1.1` (GPL-3.0). This is a blocker for a closed-source release.**\n\n- GPL-3.0 is strong copyleft. If you distribute a product that links to or bundles `somelib`, the combined work generally has to be licensed under GPL-3.0. That means offering your source to recipients, which you can't do if the product is closed source.\n- GPL-3.0 also has patent-license and anti-\"tivoization\" terms. These make it a poor fit for embedded or locked-down devices.\n- If the product is a hosted service that users never receive a copy of, the GPL's distribution trigger may not apply. That is different from AGPL, which would apply. Check how you actually ship before relying on this.\n\n**Options for `somelib`:**\n1. Replace it with a permissively licensed alternative, or write the functionality yourself.\n2. Ask the author whether they sell a commercial license or dual-license it.\n3. Open-source your product under GPL-3.0 if that's acceptable.\n4. Have counsel confirm whether your use counts as distribution or linking. For example, a separate process invoked over a CLI is treated differently from an in-process import.\n\n**Fine to ship:**\n- `requests` (Apache-2.0): keep the license text and NOTICE file, and note any changes you make to it.\n- `left-pad` (MIT): keep the copyright and license notice.\n\n**Limits of this scan:**\n- It only reads the license labels in deps.txt. I haven't verified them against the actual packages.\n- It covers only the packages listed here, not their transitive dependencies, which may carry their own licenses. Run a full dependency scan on the resolved tree before release.\n- This isn't legal advice. Have counsel sign off on the GPL decision.\n\nI can run a deeper scan, such as the dependency-auditor on the transitive tree or a check for CVEs, if you want one.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}